      "text": "Per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) have previously been formulated as integral components in many industrial and consumer products due to their remarkable properties, including water and oil resistance, as well as exceptional thermal and chemical stability [ 1 ]. These attributes make PFAS invaluable in applications ranging from firefighting foams to non-stick coatings, contributing significantly to technological advancements. Various PFAS compounds have been detected in numerous environmental matrices [ 2 ], raising significant concerns due to their persistence, bioaccumulation, and potential adverse health effects such as cancer development and thyroid hormone destruction [ 3 ]. PFAS contamination in environmental water bodies is particularly alarming as it poses risks to both the ecosystem and human health [ 4 ]. A recent study found PFAS, including PFOA (mean range 2.8 \u2013 17.7 ppt) and PFOS (mean range 5.8 \u2013 19.7 ppt), in Australian es - tuaries, with higher concentrations in creek water (CW) than in river water [ 5 ], highlighting their widespread presence in aquatic environ - ments. Among the various sources of PFAS, aqueous film-forming foam (AFFF) is notable, particularly when considering that PFAS have been formulated as the key ingredients before. AFFF has been extensively used for firefighting in situations involving flammable liquid fires, due"
