      "text": "and reduces environmental impact [ 15 , 19 ]. Compared to other tech - nologies such as electrochemical oxidation, photochemical degradation, plasma treatment, and microwave-based processes, ultrasonication may also yield fewer ultrashort-chain by-products. Nonetheless, challenges remain around operational parameters, energy input and scalability, as discussed in earlier work [ 17 ], beyond the ionic background. In addition to these controllable factors, water matrix properties such as ionic composition, conductivity, and total organic carbon (TOC) can also play a critical role in determining degradation outcomes. Given that seawater (SW), freshwater (e.g., CW), and groundwater (GW) exhibit distinct ionic profiles [ 20 ], understanding how these matrices affect PFAS degradation is crucial for optimising treatment methods across diverse water types and advancing their scale-up for real-world applications. Building on our previous work on ultrasonication of PFAS, AFFF, and FF [ 10 ], this study investigates the impact of ionic composition and water chemistry on the ultrasonication degradation of PFAS by spiking perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA), perfluorooctane sulfonate (PFOS), 6:2 fluorotelomer sulfonate (6:2 FTS), AFFF, and FF in SW and CW. Addi - tionally, a real-world PFAS-contaminated GW sample was treated,"
